e \"family\" here: models = meter size × accuracy class × flow-rate variant. That's ~36 variants. How to model?\n\nOptions:\n- model_family.models with model_id like \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\", etc.\n- matrix_tables preserving the table as-is (recommended: \"EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\").\n\nGiven the complexity (36 rows), I think the cleanest approach: emit matrix_tables for Table 2 (two parts, page 3 & 4 — or combine into one table \"General characteristics of the family of instruments\"). And populate model_family with each row as a model variant. model_level characteristics with per-model values for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class.\n\nModel IDs: something like \"DN25/2-a\"? Better human-readable: \"DN25 class 2 (Q3=10 m³/h)\". Hmm. Let me define model_id like \"DN25-C2-v1\", \"DN25-C2-v2\". Or \"DN25 class 2, Q3=10\". I'll use model_id = \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\" style: `{size}-class{class}-Q3={q3}`. For DN200 there are two class-2 rows with different Q3 (630 and 1000) so Q3 disambiguates. For DN100 class 1: Q3=160 and 250 → distinct. DN300: Q3=1600 both classes 2 and 1 — but class differs, so \"DN300-class2-Q3=1600\" vs \"DN300-class1-Q3=1600\" — distinct. DN350-600: \"1 or 2\" — model_id \"DN350-class1or2-Q3=2500\". Hmm, that's clunky but fine.\n\nAlternatively keep model_id as size+class+variant number: e.g., \"DN25-2-a\". Let me use a readable scheme: \"DN25 class 2 (Q3=10)\"... spaces and equals in IDs are fine.\n\nLet me go with: model_id: \"DN25/2/Q3=10\"? I'll pick format: `DN25 class 2 (Q3=10 m³/h)`? Too verbose. \n\nSimpler: model_id = \"DN25-C2-a\", \"DN25-C2-b\", with label describing. Schema has model_id and label. So:\n- model_id: \"DN25-C2-a\", label: \"DN25, accuracy class 2, Q3=10 m³/h\"\n- attributes: {meter_size: \"DN25\", accuracy_class: 2}\n\nThat's clean. But then model_level values reference model_id. Good.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: use model_id that encodes everything: \"DN25-C2-10\" where 10 is Q3. Hmm, ambiguity if two variants share Q3 and class — doesn't happen. Let me verify uniqueness: \n\nDN25: (2,10), (2,16) ✓\nDN40: (2,25), (2,40) ✓\nDN50: (2,40), (2,63) ✓\nDN65: (2,63), (2,100), (1,100) ✓\nDN80: (2,100), (2,160), (1,100), (1,160) ✓\nDN100: (2,160), (2,250), (1,160), (1,250) ✓\nDN125: (2,250), (2,400), (1,250), (1,400) ✓\nDN150: (2,400), (2,630), (1,400), (1,630) ✓\nDN200: (2,630), (1,630), (2,1000) ✓\nDN250: (1,1000) ✓\nDN300: (2,1600), (1,1600) ✓\nDN350: (1or2, 2500) ✓\nDN400: (1or2, 4000) ✓\nDN450: (1or2, 4000) ✓\nDN500: (1or2, 6300) ✓\nDN600: (1or2, 6300) ✓\n\nAll unique with (size, class, Q3).\n\nI'll use model_id format: \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\". For \"1 or 2\": \"DN350-class1or2-Q3=2500\". These are readable.\n\nHmm, 36 models is a lot of JSON, but it's the right thing. Let me enumerate all rows with numeric values:\n\n1. DN25, 2, 0.025, 0.04, 10, 12.5, 400\n2. DN25, 2, 0.04, 0.064, 16, 20, 400\n3. DN40, 2, 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.3, 400\n4. DN40, 2, 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n5. DN50, 2, 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n6. DN50, 2, 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n7. DN65, 2, 0.1575, 0.25, 63, 78.75, 400\n8. DN65, 2, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n9. DN65, 1, 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250\n10. DN80, 2, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n11. DN80, 2, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n12. DN80, 1, 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n13. DN80, 1, 0.64, 1.02, 160, 200, 250\n14. DN100, 2, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n15. DN100, 2, 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400\n16. DN100, 1, 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n17. DN100, 1, 1.6, 2.56, 250, 312.5, 250\n\nWait — row 17. Let me recheck DN100 row 4: `<td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1,6</td><td>250</td><td>312,5</td><td>250</td>`. Cells: class=1, Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio=250. So Q1=1, Q2=1.6. Hmm, 1.6×Q1 = 1.6×1 = 1.6 ✓, ratio 250/1 = 250 ✓. So row 17: Q1=1, Q2=1.6.\n\nHmm wait, but then rows 16 and 17 both have Q1=1, Q2=1.6, differing only in Q3/Q4/ratio. Row 16: Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio=160. Row 17: Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio=250. OK.\n\n18. DN125, 2